---
import LessonLayout from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import ClaimLabel from '../../components/ClaimLabel.astro';
import Cite from '../../components/learn/Cite.astro';
import SimPanel from '../../components/learn/SimPanel.astro';
import { BANK_LESSON_ENTITIES, BANK_OPENING_TEXT, BANK_STEPS, LESSON_CAPITAL_RATIO, bankCounterRows, bankLessonOpening, firstLoanPayment } from '../../lib/learn/banks';
import { BANK_SOURCES, LESSONS_REVIEWED, LESSONS_REVIEWED_LABEL, numberer } from '../../lib/learn/lesson-sources';
import { counters } from '../../lib/learn/sim';
import { formatUSD } from '../../lib/calc/format';
import { href } from '../../lib/url';
import '../../styles/learn.css';

const n = numberer(BANK_SOURCES);
const opening = bankLessonOpening();
const pay = firstLoanPayment();
const actions = [
  { id: 'lend', label: 'A bank lends to its customer' },
  { id: 'pay', label: 'A customer pays the other' },
  { id: 'repay', label: 'Repay loan principal' },
  { id: 'interest', label: 'Pay loan interest' },
  { id: 'write-off', label: 'The bank writes off and forgives a loan' },
];
---
<LessonLayout
  pageTitle="How Banks Create Money: Loan and Deposit Simulator"
  image="lesson-how-banks-create-money"
  about={['Money creation', 'Bank lending', 'Bank reserves', 'Bank capital']}
  title="How Banks Create Money"
  description="Make a loan and watch a new deposit appear, then follow the payment between banks, repayment with interest, a default and write-off, a reserve shortfall and a capital rule, on balance sheets that always balance."
  lead="When a bank lends, it does not hand over money someone else deposited. It writes a new deposit into the borrower's account. Step through a loan from start to finish on real accounting, and see what limits how much banks can lend."
  reviewed={LESSONS_REVIEWED}
  minutes={15}
>
  <section class="prose lesson-section" aria-labelledby="short">
    <h2 id="short">The short version</h2>
    <p class="claim-line"><ClaimLabel kind="fact" /> <span class="small muted">As central banks describe it</span></p>
    <p>
      "Whenever a bank makes a loan, it simultaneously creates a matching deposit in the
      borrower's bank account, thereby creating new money." In other words, "bank lending
      creates deposits," not the other way around.<Cite ids={['boe-2014']} number={n} />
      Federal Reserve economists put it the same way: "When banks make loans to nonbanks or
      buy assets, such as marketable securities, from nonbanks they create new bank
      deposits."<Cite ids={['feds-2022']} number={n} />
    </p>
    <p>
      Banks still need something else to settle payments with each other: reserves, their
      balances at the Federal Reserve. Reserve balances "are a liability of the Federal
      Reserve," and "only the Federal Reserve, as the central bank, can create
      reserves."<Cite ids={['fed-iorb-faq']} number={n} /> The simulation below shows both
      kinds of money at work.
    </p>
  </section>

  <SimPanel lesson="banks" heading="Step through a loan" entities={BANK_LESSON_ENTITIES} opening={opening} openingText={BANK_OPENING_TEXT} steps={BANK_STEPS.length} counters={bankCounterRows(counters(opening))} actions={actions} capitalRatio={LESSON_CAPITAL_RATIO} />

  <section class="prose lesson-section" aria-labelledby="steps">
    <h2 id="steps">What happens at each step</h2>
    <p class="small muted">Amounts are illustrative. The ledger checks after every step that each balance sheet balances and that every asset is someone else's liability.</p>
    <ol>
      {BANK_STEPS.map((s) => <li><strong>{s.title}.</strong> {s.text}</li>)}
    </ol>
    <p>
      The repayment step uses the same engine as the <a href={href('calculators/amortization/')}>loan amortization calculator</a>:
      $1,000 at 12 percent a year over 12 months means a monthly payment of {formatUSD(pay.payment)}, of which {formatUSD(pay.interest)} is the first month's interest.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose lesson-section" aria-labelledby="limits">
    <h2 id="limits">What limits lending</h2>
    <p>Banks cannot lend without limit. The simulation shows three constraints on the bank's side, each with its usual name; the last part of this section adds the borrowers' side.</p>
    <h3>Capital</h3>
    <p class="claim-line"><ClaimLabel kind="fact" /></p>
    <p>
      A bank's equity absorbs its losses, and regulators require a minimum. US banks must keep
      "a common equity tier 1 capital ratio of 4.5 percent," "a tier 1 capital ratio of 6
      percent," "a total capital ratio of 8 percent" and "a leverage ratio of 4 percent," among
      other rules.<Cite ids={['cfr-217-10']} number={n} /> Most of these ratios weigh assets by
      their risk.
    </p>
    <p class="claim-line"><ClaimLabel kind="model" /> <span class="small muted">A hypothetical rule for teaching</span></p>
    <p>
      The simulation uses its own rule instead: equity of at least 5 percent of total assets.
      That number is a hypothetical constraint chosen for teaching, not a regulatory minimum.
      Real minimums are set on regulatory capital rather than plain equity, and three of the
      four above weigh assets by their risk.
    </p>
    <h3>Liquidity</h3>
    <p class="claim-line"><ClaimLabel kind="fact" /></p>
    <p>
      When a borrower pays someone at another bank, the banks settle in reserves, for example
      over Fedwire, where a payment is "immediate, final, and irrevocable once
      processed."<Cite ids={['fed-fedwire']} number={n} /> A bank short of reserves can borrow
      them: from other banks overnight, at the federal funds rate, "the interest rate at which
      depository institutions lend reserve balances to other depository institutions
      overnight,"<Cite ids={['fed-omo']} number={n} /> a market in which "certain other entities,
      primarily government-sponsored enterprises," also lend;<Cite ids={['nyfed-effr']} number={n} />
      or from the Federal Reserve's discount window, which lets depository institutions
      "borrow from Federal Reserve Banks after executing legal agreements and pledging
      collateral."<Cite ids={['fed-discount']} number={n} /> As of October 29, 2026 the primary
      credit rate is 4.0 percent.<Cite ids={['fed-implementation-2026-10']} number={n} />
    </p>
    <h3>Credit risk</h3>
    <p class="claim-line"><ClaimLabel kind="fact" /></p>
    <p>
      A loan that will not be repaid is charged off: "removed from balance sheet because of
      uncollectability."<Cite ids={['fdic-qbp']} number={n} /> In practice a bank sets aside an
      allowance for expected losses in advance, through provisions that are an expense, and
      charges the loan off against that allowance; the reporting rules forbid charging
      uncollectible loans directly to retained earnings.<Cite ids={['ffiec-glossary']} number={n} /> Either way the
      loss reduces the bank's equity, which is all the simulation shows: it takes the loss
      straight to equity, one of the simplifications listed below.
    </p>
    <p>
      A charge-off is the bank's accounting entry, not a release of the debt. "It does not
      eliminate your obligation to the bank": unless the bank forgives or cancels the debt, the
      borrower still owes it, and the bank may try to collect it or sell it to a collection
      agency.<Cite ids={['occ-charge-off']} number={n} /> The simulation is simpler here too: it
      also takes the loan off the borrower's balance sheet, as if the bank had forgiven it.
    </p>
    <h3>Borrowers and interest rates</h3>
    <p class="claim-line"><ClaimLabel kind="fact" /></p>
    <p>
      The three constraints above sit on the bank's balance sheet. Lending also depends on
      borrowers. A bank lends only where it expects to be repaid, since it "needs to gauge its
      credit risk (whether it will be paid back in full when an investment period ends)," and
      only as much as creditworthy households and businesses want to borrow at the
      prevailing interest rate. Interest rates, which the Federal Reserve influences, shape
      that demand: "higher interest rates in the economy might dissuade a business from
      taking out a bank loan."<Cite ids={['stlfed-2021']} number={n} /> In the simulation a
      loan is made whenever you choose, so these limits do not appear.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="sidebar-model" aria-labelledby="multiplier">
    <h2 id="multiplier">The money multiplier: a historical model</h2>
    <p class="claim-line"><ClaimLabel kind="model" /> <span class="small muted">Shown for history; not how money is created today</span></p>
    <p>
      Older textbooks describe banks as lending out a multiple of their reserves: each bank
      keeps a required fraction of deposits as reserves and lends the rest, so a dollar of
      reserves becomes several dollars of deposits. In the United States the required fraction
      is now zero: "the Board reduced reserve requirement ratios to zero percent effective
      March 26, 2020," which "eliminated reserve requirements for all depository
      institutions."<Cite ids={['fed-reserve-requirements']} number={n} />
    </p>
    <p>
      The Bank of England calls the money multiplier "a useful way of introducing money and
      banking in economic textbooks" that "is not an accurate description of how money is
      created in reality."<Cite ids={['boe-2014']} number={n} /> A 2021 article published by the
      Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis, written by Jane Ihrig and Gretchen Weinbach of the
      Federal Reserve Board and Scott Wolla of the St. Louis Fed's economic education staff,
      calls it "an outdated concept."<Cite ids={['stlfed-2021']} number={n} />
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose lesson-section" aria-labelledby="simplified">
    <h2 id="simplified">What the simulation leaves out</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Two small banks and a handful of customers stand in for the whole system. The amounts are illustrative.</li>
      <li>Interest and losses go straight to equity: there is no income statement, no loss allowance and no tax.</li>
      <li>A write-off also cancels the borrower's debt. In reality a charged-off loan is still owed unless the bank forgives it.</li>
      <li>The capital rule (equity of at least 5 percent of total assets) is a hypothetical one for teaching, not a regulatory minimum.</li>
      <li>Loans are made whenever you choose. Real lending also depends on creditworthy borrowers wanting loans at the prevailing interest rate.</li>
      <li>Payments settle at once; there is no intraday credit, and no clearing banks or dealers.</li>
      <li>Banks here hold only reserves, Treasury securities and loans, and no cash in the vault.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="key-ideas" aria-labelledby="key-ideas">
    <h2 id="key-ideas">Key ideas</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>A bank loan creates a deposit, which is new money; repaying the loan destroys it.</li>
      <li>Banks settle payments with each other in reserves, which only the Federal Reserve creates.</li>
      <li>A bank short of reserves borrows them, from other banks or from the Fed's discount window.</li>
      <li>Losses come out of a bank's equity, and capital rules limit how much it can lend on its equity.</li>
      <li>Lending also depends on creditworthy borrowers wanting loans at the prevailing interest rate, which the Federal Reserve influences.</li>
      <li>Reserve requirements have been zero since March 2020; the money multiplier is a historical model.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="prose lesson-section" aria-labelledby="related">
    <h2 id="related">Related</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href={href('learn/what-is-money/')}>What Is Money?</a>: deposits, reserves and other kinds of money compared.</li>
      <li><a href={href('learn/how-money-flows/')}>How Money Flows</a>: payments, taxes and the Federal Reserve across the whole system.</li>
      <li><a href={href('calculators/amortization/')}>Loan amortization calculator</a>: a loan's full repayment schedule.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="prose lesson-section" aria-labelledby="sources">
    <h2 id="sources">Sources</h2>
    <p class="small">Every source was checked on {LESSONS_REVIEWED_LABEL}. Rates and rules are stated as of that date.</p>
    <ol class="source-list small">
      {BANK_SOURCES.map((s) => (
        <li id={`src-${s.id}`}>{s.publisher}, <a href={s.url}>{s.title}</a>, {s.date}.</li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </section>
</LessonLayout>

<script>
  import '../../scripts/learn/banks';
</script>
